      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the probability that two randomly chosen members from a club of 10 members (5 boys and 5 girls) are both girls, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the total number of ways to choose 2 members out of 10.**\n\n   The number of ways to choose 2 members out of 10 is given by the combination formula \\( \\binom{n}{k} \\), which represents the number of ways to choose \\( k \\) elements from a set of \\( n \\) elements without regard to the order of selection. Here, \\( n = 10 \\) and \\( k = 2 \\).\n\n   \\[\n   \\binom{10}{2} = \\frac{10!}{2!(10-2)!} = \\frac{10!}{2! \\cdot 8!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9}{2 \\times 1} = 45\n   \\]\n\n   So, there are 45 possible ways to choose 2 members out of 10.\n\n2. **Calculate the number of ways to choose 2 girls out of 5.**\n\n   Similarly, the number of ways to choose 2 girls out of 5 is given by the combination formula \\( \\binom{n}{k} \\), where \\( n = 5 \\) and \\( k = 2 \\).\n\n   \\[\n   \\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5!}{2!(5-2)!} = \\frac{5!}{2! \\cdot 3!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4}{2 \\times 1} = 10\n   \\]\n\n   So, there are 10 possible ways to choose 2 girls out of 5.\n\n3. **Calculate the probability that both members chosen are girls.**\n\n   The probability is the number of favorable outcomes (choosing 2 girls) divided by the total number of outcomes (choosing any 2 members from 10).\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Probability} = \\frac{\\text{Number of ways to choose 2 girls}}{\\text{Total number of ways to choose 2 members}} = \\frac{10}{45} = \\frac{2}{9}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that both members chosen are girls is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{2}{9}}\\).",
