    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the probability that both chosen members are girls, we can use the concept of combinations and the definition of probability.\n\n1. **Calculate the total number of ways to choose 2 members out of 10:**\n\n   The number of ways to choose 2 members from 10 can be calculated using the combination formula:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{10}{2} = \\frac{10!}{2!(10-2)!} = \\frac{10!}{2! \\cdot 8!}\n   \\]\n\n   Simplify the factorials:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{10}{2} = \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8!}{2 \\times 1 \\times 8!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9}{2 \\times 1} = 45\n   \\]\n\n   So, there are 45 ways to choose 2 members from 10.\n\n2. **Calculate the number of ways to choose 2 girls out of 5:**\n\n   The number of ways to choose 2 girls from 5 can be calculated using the combination formula:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5!}{2!(5-2)!} = \\frac{5!}{2! \\cdot 3!}\n   \\]\n\n   Simplify the factorials:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5 \\times 4 \\times 3!}{2 \\times 1 \\times 3!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4}{2 \\times 1} = 10\n   \\]\n\n   So, there are 10 ways to choose 2 girls from 5.\n\n3. **Calculate the probability that both chosen members are girls:**\n\n   The probability is the ratio of the number of favorable outcomes (choosing 2 girls) to the total number of possible outcomes (choosing any 2 members):\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{both girls}) = \\frac{\\text{Number of ways to choose 2 girls}}{\\text{Total number of ways to choose 2 members}} = \\frac{10}{45}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Simplify the fraction:**\n\n   Simplify \\(\\frac{10}{45}\\) by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by their greatest common divisor, which is 5:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{10}{45} = \\frac{10 \\div 5}{45 \\div 5} = \\frac{2}{9}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that both chosen members are girls is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{2}{9}}\\).",
